    id: 1, module: "Module 1: Android Fundamentals", title: "Introduction to Android Development",
    type: "theory", duration: "25 min",
    content: `Android is the world's most widely used mobile operating system, powering over 3 billion active devices globally across smartphones, tablets, smartwatches, TVs, and cars. Built on the Linux kernel and primarily programmed in Kotlin (and Java), Android is open source and maintained by Google through the Android Open Source Project (AOSP).\n\nWhy Android Development?\n- Massive market reach: ~72% global smartphone market share (2024)\n- In Africa specifically, Android dominance exceeds 90% — the primary digital access point for most Africans\n- Google Play Store: 3.5+ million apps available\n- Diverse device landscape from $50 entry-level to $1,500 flagships\n- Strong career demand in telecoms, fintech, agritech, healthtech, and edtech\n\nAndroid Architecture Layers (from bottom to top):\n1. Linux Kernel: Hardware abstraction, drivers, security, power management\n2. Hardware Abstraction Layer (HAL): Interfaces between Android framework and hardware drivers\n3. Android Runtime (ART): Compiles app code using AOT (Ahead-Of-Time) compilation for performance\n4. Native C/C++ Libraries: OpenGL ES (graphics), SQLite (database), WebKit (browser engine)\n5. Java/Kotlin API Framework: Activity Manager, View System, Content Providers, Location Manager, etc.\n6. Applications: The apps users install and use\n\nAndroid Version History:\nAndroid follows a yearly release cycle. Each major version since Android 10 uses numeric naming (10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15). Older versions had dessert names (Oreo, Pie). The Android API Level (a number) is more important for developers than the marketing name — it determines which APIs are available.\n\nKey Terminology:\n- Activity: A single screen with a user interface (UI). A shopping app has a ProductListActivity, ProductDetailActivity, CheckoutActivity.\n- Fragment: A reusable portion of a UI that can be embedded in activities.\n- Intent: A messaging object to request an action from another component (open camera, send email, start a new Activity).\n- APK (Android Package): The installation file format for Android apps.\n- AAB (Android App Bundle): Google's modern distribution format that generates optimized APKs per device.\n- Gradle: The build system that compiles, packages, and manages dependencies in Android projects.\n- SDK (Software Development Kit): The tools, libraries, and APIs for building Android apps.\n\nAndroid Development Ecosystem:\n- Primary language: Kotlin (official since 2017, preferred over Java)\n- IDE: Android Studio (based on IntelliJ IDEA)\n- Build system: Gradle with Kotlin DSL\n- UI toolkit: Jetpack Compose (modern, declarative) or XML Views (legacy)\n- Libraries: Jetpack (ViewModel, Room, Navigation, LiveData, DataStore)`,
