    id: 2, module: "Module 1: Android Fundamentals", title: "Android Studio Setup & Project Structure",
    type: "practical", duration: "35 min",
    content: `Android Studio is the official Integrated Development Environment (IDE) for Android development. It provides everything you need to write, test, debug, and deploy Android applications — all in one environment.\n\nSystem Requirements for Android Studio:\n- Windows: 64-bit, 8GB RAM (16GB recommended), 8GB disk space, Intel or AMD processor\n- macOS: 8GB RAM, 8GB disk space\n- Linux: 8GB RAM, 8GB disk space, GNOME or KDE desktop\n- Android Emulator requires hardware virtualization (Intel VT-x / AMD-V)\n\nInstallation Steps:\n1. Download Android Studio from developer.android.com/studio\n2. Run installer — includes Android SDK, Android Virtual Device (AVD) manager, and Emulator\n3. Launch and complete the Setup Wizard — it downloads the required SDK components\n4. Configure proxy settings if needed for your network\n\nAndroid Studio Key Interface Areas:\n- Project pane (left): File and project structure navigator\n- Editor area (center): Code editor with syntax highlighting, autocompletion\n- Logcat (bottom): Real-time device logs — essential for debugging\n- Android Virtual Device (AVD) Manager: Creates and manages emulators\n- Build Variants: Debug / Release build configurations\n- Gradle panel (right): Run Gradle tasks manually\n\nAndroid Project Structure:\nmy-app/\n├── app/\n│   ├── src/\n│   │   ├── main/\n│   │   │   ├── java/com/example/myapp/  (or kotlin/)\n│   │   │   │   ├── MainActivity.kt\n│   │   │   │   └── ui/\n│   │   │   ├── res/\n│   │   │   │   ├── layout/          (XML layout files)\n│   │   │   │   ├── drawable/        (images, vector graphics)\n│   │   │   │   ├── values/          (strings.xml, colors.xml, themes.xml)\n│   │   │   │   ├── mipmap/          (launcher icons)\n│   │   │   │   └── raw/             (media files)\n│   │   │   └── AndroidManifest.xml  (app declaration)\n│   │   ├── test/                    (unit tests)\n│   │   └── androidTest/             (instrumented tests)\n│   ├── build.gradle.kts             (app module config)\n│   └── proguard-rules.pro           (code obfuscation rules)\n├── build.gradle.kts                 (project-level config)\n├── gradle.properties\n├── settings.gradle.kts\n└── local.properties                 (SDK path — DO NOT commit to git)\n\nAndroidManifest.xml — The App Declaration:\nEvery Android app must have an AndroidManifest.xml. It declares:\n- Package name (unique identifier: com.company.appname)\n- All Activities, Services, BroadcastReceivers, ContentProviders\n- Required permissions (INTERNET, CAMERA, LOCATION)\n- Minimum and target SDK versions\n- App icon, label, and theme\n\nExample AndroidManifest.xml snippet:\n<manifest xmlns:android=\"http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android\">\n  <uses-permission android:name=\"android.permission.INTERNET\" />\n  <application\n    android:icon=\"@mipmap/ic_launcher\"\n    android:label=\"@string/app_name\"\n    android:theme=\"@style/Theme.MyApp\">\n    <activity\n      android:name=\".MainActivity\"\n      android:exported=\"true\">\n      <intent-filter>\n        <action android:name=\"android.intent.action.MAIN\" />\n        <category android:name=\"android.intent.category.LAUNCHER\" />\n      </intent-filter>\n    </activity>\n  </application>\n</manifest>`,
